{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And He commanded them concerning the children of Israel</b> — Rashi explains: what is the substance of the command? It is spelled out in the second section, after the genealogy: because once Moses and Aaron were mentioned, the narrative was interrupted to teach us how they were born and to whom they traced their lineage. \"And HaShem spoke to Moses, saying: I am HaShem. Speak to Pharaoh king of Egypt all that I speak to you\" (Exodus 6:29) — this is the very utterance stated here [in our verse], \"Come, speak to Pharaoh king of Egypt.\" But since the narrative was interrupted for the sake of tracing their lineage, Scripture returned to it to begin from it anew. \"And Moses said before HaShem\" (Exodus 6:30) — this is the statement made here, \"Behold, the children of Israel have not listened to me,\" and so on. And Scripture repeated it because the narrative had been interrupted. This is the method: like a person who says, let us go back over what came first. And such is also the opinion of Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 6:13).\n\nBut this is not my opinion. Rather, when HaShem commanded Moses to speak to the children of Israel, \"Therefore say to the children of Israel\" (Exodus 6:6), and he did so, but they did not listen to him, He returned and commanded him to go to Pharaoh and command him to send them out. Then [Moses] answered: \"Behold, the children of Israel have not listened to me.\" And the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded both of them to speak to the people and to Pharaoh. Now Moses reckoned that both of them would prophesy with all the signs and that both would come to the children of Israel and to Pharaoh, and it would suffice for one of them to speak, for so is the way of all two envoys: one speaks and the second is silent, and they would be reconciled with this. Then HaShem said to him again: \"I am HaShem\" (Exodus 6:29) — I who am revealed to you alone, to speak in My great Name — \"Speak to Pharaoh king of Egypt all that I speak to you,\" for to you shall belong all the words, not to Aaron with you, and you I have made emissary to Pharaoh. Then Moses answered once more: \"Behold, I am of uncircumcised lips,\" and HaShem said to him: \"See, I have made you Elohim to Pharaoh\" (Exodus 7:1) — for you shall come before him with Aaron, and there you shall command Aaron, and Pharaoh will not hear your words, but Aaron, by your commission, will make him hear your words, just as Elohim commands the prophet, and the prophet makes His words heard and rebukes with them.\n\nNow behold, this is a great distinction for Moses, which he merited through his humility, for he was ashamed to speak with uncircumcised lips. And likewise Scripture says (Exodus 11:3): \"Moreover the man Moses was very great in the land of Egypt, in the eyes of Pharaoh's servants and in the eyes of the people\" — measure for measure, for he feared lest he be despised in their eyes.\n\nAnd Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 7:2): You shall speak once, each and every commission, exactly as you heard it from My mouth, and Aaron your brother will interpret it and explain it in Pharaoh's ears. And this is not correct at all."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The phrase 'והמשכיל יבין' does not appear in this passage; the kabbalistic formula is not invoked here. Rashi's remark paraphrased at the end corresponds to his comment on Exodus 7:2 (לא תאמר אלא פעם אחת כל שליחות ושליחות כפי ששמעת מפי ואהרן אחיך ימליצנו ויטעימנו באזני פרעה)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:04:06.768270109Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}